.TH CHTTPCLIENT_DO 3 "2026-07-11" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_do \- do an HTTP request and buffer the full response
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpclient_do(chttpcli cli,
                             const chttp_request_t *req,
                             chttpcli_response **resp_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function takes a pool slot, blocking while every slot is in use.
It then runs the HTTP request synchronously, buffers the whole response
body, and puts a chttpcli_response on the heap into *resp_out.

The function sets *resp_out to NULL at once, before it starts any
other work, and *resp_out stays NULL on every return that is not a
success. So you can always call chttpclient_resp_free(resp) after this
call, whatever ccol_retval_t it gives back, and you do not need to set
your own local pointer before the call. After a success the caller
owns *resp_out and must call chttpclient_resp_free on it at the end.

The client follows a redirect automatically. The redirect codes are
301, 302, 303, 307 and 308, and the client follows up to 50 hops. For
301, 302 and 303 it rewrites the method to a GET with no body, but
leaves HEAD as HEAD; for 307 and 308 it keeps the original method and
the original body. The Location header can be an absolute URL, a
protocol-relative reference ("//host/path"), an absolute-path
reference ("/foo"), or a general relative reference, such as "foo",
"../foo", "./foo" or "?query", and the client resolves all of them as
RFC 3986 asks. A Location value with a scheme of its own is always
absolute, as RFC 3986 SS5.2.2 says: for example "mailto:x@y",
"ftp://host/path" or any scheme other than http, https and http+unix.
Such a value resolves to itself without a change, and because this
client connects only over http, https and http+unix, the next hop
reports ccol_http_invalid_url, the same code that an unsupported scheme
in the original request URL gets. The client does not merge the
reference onto the path of the current origin as if the reference were
relative. A response with a redirect code and no Location header, or
an empty one, is not a redirect: the client delivers it as the final
response, as the Go net/http client does.

A redirect from https to http is followed by default. That matches
curl, whose CURLOPT_REDIR_PROTOCOLS permits both schemes, and the Go
net/http client.

Set chttp_request_t.prevent_tls_downgrade_on_redirect to refuse such a
redirect instead; the client then reports ccol_http_invalid_url and
opens no plaintext connection. Weigh that against the default: the
peer that sent the Location chooses the downgrade, and once a chain
leaves TLS, that hop and every later hop travel in clear.

A Location that merely inherits the scheme, such as "//host/path" or
"/path", resolves back to https and follows whatever that field holds,
and so does an upgrade from http to https, which can only add
protection.

The rule below, for AF_UNIX, is not optional and no field relaxes it.

A hop can name a Unix domain
socket ("http+unix://...") only when the hop that carried the Location
was bound to that same socket path. The client refuses every other
resolved http+unix:// target with ccol_http_invalid_url, and tries no
connection. The rule holds for every hop, judged against the hop that
the Location came from, so a chain that leaves a socket cannot come
back to it, and a chain that never used a socket cannot reach one,
whatever the length of the chain. The match of the scheme ignores case,
and the client compares the socket path after it decodes the percent
escapes, so a different spelling or a second encoding cannot get past
the rule. A relative Location on a Unix hop still resolves back onto
the socket of that hop, and the client follows it in the normal way.
Without this rule, any http or https server could redirect a caller
onto any local socket, while also controlling the target of the
request.

If the client reaches the cap of 50 hops and the response of the last
hop is itself a redirect, the client neither follows it nor delivers
it, and gives ccol_http_too_many_redirects instead. The normalization
of the dot segments ".." and "." only rewrites the path part; the
client never treats "/", ".." or "." inside a query string as path
navigation. When the request URL carries credentials (see below), the
client makes an Authorization header from them and sends that header
again on every hop that stays on the same origin. An origin is a
scheme, a host and a port: the host compares without regard to case,
and the scheme and the port compare exactly. The client drops the
header for good the first time a hop changes the origin.

The headers that you set with chttp_request_set_header follow a
redirect with two exceptions, which are the rules of curl. The client
judges every hop against the ORIGINAL request, never against the hop
before it. A hop whose scheme, host or port differs from the original
request carries none of your Authorization, Cookie, Cookie2 and
WWW-Authenticate headers. A hop whose host differs also carries none of
your Host header, and the client writes its own Host line for the new
target instead. Hosts and header names match without regard to case. A
hop that comes back to the original origin therefore carries those
headers again, and a second hop on the same foreign origin still
carries none of them. Every other header of yours follows every hop.

A 301, 302 or 303 hop rewrites the method to a GET with no body, and
removes every Content-Length, Content-Type and Expect header that the
caller set, from that hop and from every hop after it; a later 307 or
308 hop can bring a real body back, and the client then sends those
headers again. Those three headers only describe a body, and a value
that described the body of the original request is never correct on a
request with no body at all. The client sends every other header of the
caller again on every hop, without a change.

The request URL accepts http://, https:// and http+unix://, and the
support for a Unix domain socket is the same in all three tiers. The
form with the encoded socket path is
"http+unix://%2Fpath%2Fto%2Fsock/path", which matches the convention of
requests-unixsocket in Python. For the two network forms the client
accepts a plain hostname, an IPv4 literal, and an IPv6 literal inside
brackets ("https://[::1]:8443/path"). A URL can
also carry credentials
("http://user:pass@host/path"), which the client turns into an
"Authorization: Basic ..." header for you, unless the request already
sets its own Authorization header. This does not apply to
http+unix://, which has no convention for userinfo. The client also
finds a "#fragment" at the end of a URL and drops it: it never sends a
fragment to a server.

An empty port, as in "http://host:/", names the default port of the
scheme (RFC 3986 section 3.2.3), as it does in curl, and port 0 is
refused. A fully qualified hostname with one trailing dot, as in
"https://www.example.com./", is resolved and sent in the Host header
exactly as written, while TLS uses the name without that dot for SNI
and for the check of the certificate, as curl and Go do. The keep-alive
cache matches hosts without regard to case, but a name with a trailing
dot and the same name without it are two origins, because the name
without the dot can resolve through a search domain to another host.

A server can send one response header name on several lines. See
chttpclient_resp_header for the combined value, and
chttpclient_resp_header_at for each line separately. A redirect with
more than one Location line goes to the first one, as curl and the Go
net/http client do.

The client builds the request-target of the request line from the path
and the query of the URL, and from those of every Location that it
follows. It percent-encodes as "%XX" every byte that RFC 3986 does not
allow there literally: the space, the control bytes, DEL, every byte
of 0x80 and above, and the characters '"', '<', '>', '\\', '^', '`',
\&'{', '|', '}', '[' and ']'. An existing "%XX" escape is kept as it is
and never encoded a second time. A '%' that is not followed by two hex
digits makes the URL invalid, in your URL and in a Location alike; Go's
net/url rejects the same input. For example, a Location of "/my file"
becomes the request-target "/my%20file".

A host name can resolve to several addresses. The client connects
with Happy Eyeballs (RFC 8305), as the default dialer of Go does: it
takes the addresses in the order that the resolver gives, which is the
order of RFC 6724, and alternates the address families, starting with
the family of the first address. It starts the first address at once,
and the next one when the attempts in flight have given no result for
250 ms, or at once when an attempt fails. The first attempt that
connects wins, and the client closes every other one; it reports
ccol_http_connection_failed only after every address failed. The
connect timeout is one budget for the whole race; when it runs out,
the connect ends with ccol_timed_out. So an address that drops its
packets costs about 250 ms rather than the connect timeout, and the
name "localhost", which a standard /etc/hosts lists as ::1 before
127.0.0.1, reaches a server that listens on 127.0.0.1 alone.
chttpclient_do starts the next address exactly at 250 ms;
chttpclient_do_async and chttpclient_do_pooled start it between
250 ms and 350 ms after the one before it.

A server can answer before it reads the full request, for example with
a 401 from a check of credentials or a 413 from a size limit, and can
then close the connection or stop reading. Each tier monitors the
connection for such an answer while it sends the request, and gives
that answer, as curl and the net/http client of Go do. The rules are:
  - The client drops an interim (1xx) response and continues the send.
  - A final response of 300 or above stops the send when its header
    block has arrived; the client does not send the remaining part of
    the request.
  - A final response of 200 to 299 lets the send continue while the
    response arrives, because a server can stream its answer while it
    reads the request. A complete response stops the send.
  - A write that fails because the peer went away stops the send, and
    the client then gives the answer that the peer sent before it
    went, if there is one.
The client never reuses a connection whose response started before the
client sent the full request.

On Linux, a reset discards the data that the client did not read, so
the client gives an answer only when it reads that answer before a
reset arrives. chttpclient_do waits for readability during each wait of
the send. chttpclient_do_async and chttpclient_do_pooled look for an
answer at these times:
  - When a write would block.
  - After a write, when 32 KiB or more went out since the last look.
A response that arrives while a write of chttpclient_do_async or
chttpclient_do_pooled waits for space in the socket is seen by these
functions within 100 ms.

Each socket of the client is close-on-exec, and so is each file that
the TLS layer of the client reads, so a process that the application
starts gets no connection of the client.

The server can send interim informational responses with a 1xx
status. The client drops each such response and continues to read on
the same connection for the real response, so a caller never gets a
1xx status as the final answer. "103 Early Hints" of RFC 8297 is the
most frequent example, and it can come before an ordinary response,
whatever the value of req->expect_continue.

The client does not accept an infinite sequence of interim responses:
after it drops 64 interim responses in sequence on one hop,
chttpclient_do stops and gives ccol_http_transfer_aborted. This limit
matters because the request timeout is 0 by default, and 0 means no
timeout.

The client does not drop "101 Switching Protocols". A 101 answers an
Upgrade request, which this client never sends, and the bytes after a
101 belong to a different protocol, so a 101 fails the request with
ccol_http_transfer_aborted in every tier, as in curl.

One header line of a response can be up to 64 KiB long, and the whole
header block up to 64 KiB and 100 lines, while the status line stays
within 8 KiB. A response past any of those limits gives
ccol_http_transfer_aborted, and the same limits hold in every tier.

If req->expect_continue is true and the request has a body, the client
sends only the headers of the request first, and then waits up to one
second for the first byte of a response from the server. The second
bounds only that wait: a response that starts inside it is read to its
end, bounded only by the request timeout, however long its rest takes.
The wait ends in one of these ways:

  - The server sends a "100 Continue" interim response. The client
    then sends the body and reads the real final response in the
    normal way. A response that arrives together with the
    "100 Continue" is read before the body goes out, under the
    rules for an answer that arrives while the request is sent
    (see above): a final response of 300 or above, or a complete
    one, ends the request with no byte of the body sent.
  - The server sends some OTHER interim 1xx status while the client
    waits, such as "103 Early Hints" before "100 Continue". The
    client drops it and waits on for the first byte of the next
    response, inside the same budget of one second.
  - The server answers with a final status instead, for example to
    reject the request and ask for no body. The client gives that
    response to the caller as it is and never sends the body, and it
    closes the connection afterwards and never reuses it. This holds
    for a final response that is still arriving when the second ends.
  - The wait of one second ends before any byte of a response. The
    client then takes the server for one that does not support this
    mechanism, sends the body and reads the final response in the
    normal way.

The cap of 64 dropped interim responses in a row applies separately to
two phases: the wait for "100 Continue", and the read of the final
response after it. A "100 Continue" is a real outcome, and so is the
end of the wait with no response; neither is a dropped response, and
each one sets the count back to zero. One hop can therefore drop up to
64 interim responses during the wait for "100 Continue", and then up to
64 more during the read of the final response; the total is not 64.

expect_continue has no effect on a request with no body. Every tier
honors it in the same way: chttpclient_do_async and everything on top
of it use the same protocol with three outcomes that the text above
describes, but make it part of their own dispatch, which does not
block, instead of a wait that blocks.

Once the client has got a real "100 Continue" interim response and
written the body on that connection, it reports a later failure on
that connection directly, and never tries the request again on a
different connection, even for a connection from the keep-alive idle
pool. The server showed that it was alive and that it accepted the
body, so a retry would send the body a second time, on a different
connection, and a request that is not idempotent could then run two
times. "Connection reuse" in chttpclient(7) gives the general rule
that lets the client try a reused connection again one time; this case
is the one exception to that rule.

The response body in the buffer has no size limit by default. Call
chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size to cap it; the client then
gives ccol_msg_too_large for a response above that cap instead of
buffering the whole body.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli       The client handle. It must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
req       The request to run. It must not be NULL.
resp_out  The output parameter. It must not be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success
       The request finished, and *resp_out is valid.

ccol_invalid_args
       An argument is NULL; or cli is CHTTPCLI_INVALID, or a stale
       handle of a client that is already destroyed; or a request
       header holds a CR byte or an LF byte inside it, and the same
       for body.content_type on a request with a body; or a request
       header has the name "Transfer-Encoding"; or, on a request that
       carries a body (POST, PUT or PATCH), a request header with the
       name "Content-Length" holds a bad value. A good value is a
       plain unsigned string of decimal digits, with no leading '+' or
       '-' and no whitespace inside it, and it must also match the
       real length of the body that the client sends. See
       chttp_request_set_header(3).

ccol_not_enough_memory
       An allocation of memory failed.

ccol_timed_out
       The request timeout or the connect timeout fired.

ccol_not_permitted
       The client is being destroyed, and accepts no new request.

ccol_http_invalid_url
       The URL is malformed; or it uses an unsupported scheme (the
       client supports only http://, https:// and http+unix://); or
       its host, port or userinfo part is missing or not valid.

       The host must match the uri-host rule of RFC 3986 section
       3.2.2, that is, it must be one of these:
         - A name of letters, digits, '-', '.', '_', '~', the
           sub-delims !$&'()*+,;= and %XX escapes.
         - A bracketed IPv6 address or IPvFuture literal.
       A space, a tab, a control byte, a byte of 0x80 or above, a
       backslash, and a bracket that the authority does not close all
       make the URL invalid. Give a host name outside ASCII in its
       punycode (IDNA) form. The client does not support an IPv6 zone
       identifier.

       Credentials in the URL can hold only the characters of the
       userinfo rule, %XX escapes and an unescaped '@' in the
       password. A user name that holds a ':' after the client decodes
       its escapes, as in "http://a%3Ab:pw@host/", has no valid Basic
       credential (see chttp_basic_auth(3)), so the URL is then also
       invalid.

       A CR byte or an LF byte in the path or in the query, and a '%'
       in the path or the query that is not followed by two hex
       digits, also make the URL invalid. Without these checks, the
       client would copy such a byte into the Host header or the
       request line, and the URL could then inject more header lines,
       or smuggle a full second request. Each Location that the client
       follows goes through the same rules.

       Or, for http+unix://, the URL gives a socket path that is too
       long for sockaddr_un.sun_path. The client also gives this code
       when a redirect resolves to a http+unix:// target and the hop
       that carried the Location was not bound to it (see
       DESCRIPTION).

ccol_http_host_resolution_failed
       The DNS resolution of the target host failed. The client never
       gives this code for http+unix://, which has no DNS step.

ccol_http_connection_failed
       The client could not make the connection: every address of the
       host refused it or failed, or a http+unix:// socket path does
       not exist. A socket error on a connection that the client
       already made is ccol_http_transfer_aborted instead.

ccol_http_too_many_redirects
       The chain of redirects went past 50 hops.

ccol_http_tls_handshake_failed
       The TLS handshake failed for a reason other than the
       verification of a certificate.

ccol_http_tls_cert_verification_failed
       The client could not verify the certificate of the peer or its
       hostname.

ccol_http_tls_cert_load_failed
       The client could not read the client certificate, the key or
       the CA bundle path of the configuration, or the TLS layer could
       not load or parse one of them.

ccol_http_transfer_aborted
       The connection failed during the transfer, or the server sent
       a malformed HTTP/1.1 response. A response with ambiguous
       framing is malformed, and these responses have ambiguous
       framing:
         - A response with a Content-Length and a Transfer-Encoding
           (RFC 7230 section 3.3.3).
         - A response that applies "chunked" more than one time
           (section 3.3.1). The client reads the codings of all
           Transfer-Encoding lines as one list.
       A Transfer-Encoding without "chunked" and without a
       Content-Length is not ambiguous: the client reads such a body
       until the connection closes. A chunk-size line is also
       malformed when its chunk extension does not obey the grammar
       of RFC 9112 section 7.1.1, for example when it holds a control
       byte.

       Or a streaming chttpcli_write_fn gave back a value that is not
       the number of bytes that it got.

       Over https://, this code also covers a response whose body
       ends at the close of the connection (no Content-Length and no
       chunked framing) when the server closes without a TLS
       close_notify alert. Without that alert, the client cannot tell
       the end of the body from a connection that an attacker or a
       failure stopped too early, so it reports the response as
       truncated. A body with a length or with chunked framing does
       not need the alert.

       A connection that fails while the client sends the request
       gives this code only when the client found no answer from the
       server; see DESCRIPTION. A "101 Switching Protocols" response
       also gives this code.

ccol_msg_too_large
       The response body went past the cap of
       chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size. The client gives this
       code only after you set that cap.

ccol_unexpected_failure
       Any other internal failure that the codes above do not cover.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The library leaves the disposition of SIGPIPE to the application and
never changes it, in any tier. No write of the client raises that
signal: every write to a socket, a TLS record and the close_notify of
a TLS teardown included, is a send(2) with MSG_NOSIGNAL. A server that
closes or resets the connection while the client writes a request
therefore fails that request, and never ends the process.
.fi
